    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Outside of formal language classrooms, the resources to learn a foreign language are limited. Many people traveling abroad turn to technology for help, and take advantage of free language learning apps like Duolingo. However, users of these programs often feel like they can’t transfer what they’ve learned from the app to a real-world interaction where they want to accomplish a specific communicative goal. Research has pointed to contextualized practice to help with transfer, and the pedagogical approach of Task-Based Language Teaching (TBLT) lends itself well to this practice. However, the most popular language learning apps do not use TBLT in their approach to language teaching because of the difficulty of incorporating such a complex instructional system into their simplified instructional mechanics. One area of technology that can easily incorporate TBLT into its instructional approach is video games. Research supports the benefits of video games for learning as well as the relevance of TBLT in gaming. Several language games currently use TLBT as their pedagogical model; however, of those that are available to the public, none incorporate contextualized tasks into a larger cohesive game narrative. To address this gap, I propose to incorporate authentic, contextualized, and relevant tasks for the motivated traveler within the engaging narrative of a text-based adventure computer game. My solution focuses on 1) addressing essential language for travel and 2) increasing learner engagement by contextualizing learning through story narrative.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
